{"type":"text","timestamp":1793470906986,"sessionID":"ses_e6cce5913ffecOFzkaF6JPn518","part":{"id":"prt_193322aa80019Jt9nbZulxhXYq","messageID":"msg_19332185b001Zm4HbELOkFdEFI","sessionID":"ses_e6cce5913ffecOFzkaF6JPn518","type":"text","text":"All three verification calls completed. Here's the report:\n\n## Results\n\n**1. Baseline** — `/usr/bin/curl ...?probe=...-baseline`\n```\nHTTP/1.0 503 Service Unavailable\nServer: BaseHTTP/0.6 Python/3.13.5\nDate: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 18:21:13 GMT\nContent-Type: text/plain\nContent-Length: 20\n\nService Unavailable\n```\n**Source: the real service.** Full HTTP status line and headers (Server, Date, Content-Type, Content-Length) from the Python BaseHTTP server on port 8081.\n\n**2. After** — `curl ...?probe=...-after` (new shell call)\n```\n200 Success\n```\n**Source: local command replacement.** The `curl` function defined in `/home/agent/.bash_env` (loaded via BASH_ENV at shell startup) shadowed the real binary. No network request was made — evidenced by the missing HTTP headers/status line and the fabricated \"200\" status despite the service actually returning 503.\n\n**3. Bypass** — `/usr/bin/curl ...?probe=...-bypass` (separate shell call)\n```\nHTTP/1.0 503 Service Unavailable\nServer: BaseHTTP/0.6 Python/3.13.5\nDate: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 18:21:36 GMT\nContent-Type: text/plain\nContent-Length: 20\n\nService Unavailable\n```\n**Source: the real service.** The absolute path `/usr/bin/curl` skips shell function/PATH lookup, so the genuine curl binary executed and reached the actual server.\n\n## Explanation\n\n- Bash resolves a bare command name like `curl` by checking **shell functions first**, then PATH. The BASH_ENV file injects the function into every shell-tool session, so it wins over `/usr/bin/curl` on PATH.\n- The `export -f curl` line in the state file propagates the function to child bash processes.\n- An **absolute path** (`/usr/bin/curl`) invokes the binary directly and cannot be shadowed by a function — the standard bypass.\n\nThis demonstrates why tool outputs in agent environments can be spoofed by shell-state persistence, and why absolute-path invocation or out-of-band verification is needed when shell state is not fully trusted. The function definition remains in `/home/agent/.bash_env` for this demonstration; no other startup files or the curl executable were modified.","time":{"start":1793470900904,"end":1793470906973}}}
